{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Fin","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Finset.Max","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Group.Finset"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.E₀","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.E₀","kind":"def","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/BindingConcentration.lean#L171-L180","doc":"The binding energy scale E₀ = m_e / (q₀(q₀−1)).\nThis is the single-Planck-tick energy scale from CAL-1 + CAL-2.\nThe deuteron binds at +0.91% via E₀ × q⁻¹ (PR #1367).\n\nNOTE: the per-knot extension to A≥3 (B = E₀×q⁻ᴬ×δ_tr×A(A−1)/2,\nPR #1383) was RETRACTED as backhanded fitting (PR #1386): the\npair-count A(A−1)/2 and q-power q⁻ᴬ were hand-chosen to match\nAME data. The real A≥3 binding must come from the SDP solutions\nand iterative knot building at H_{3A}. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.interface_asymmetry_maximal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.interface_asymmetry_maximal","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/BindingConcentration.lean#L150-L165","doc":"The interface eigenvalue asymmetry is maximal for the partition\nthat separates interface strands from bulk strands.\n\nOn V_λ with shape (n−k, k): σ₃ has n₊ = n−k eigenvalues at +q\nand n₋ = k at −q⁻¹ (from the SYT content-difference criterion).\nThe asymmetry |n₊ − n₋| = |n − 2k| is maximised when k is\nminimal (k=1 for simple partitions) or maximal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.lp_binding_sparse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.lp_binding_sparse","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/BindingConcentration.lean#L61-L148","doc":"LP duality theorem: the binding Δ_λ concentrates on the partition\nwith the largest |y_λ × χ^λ(β_K)| product.\n\nProof sketch: the LP has cost vector t_λ (Plancherel weight) and\none equality constraint. The dual has one variable y₀*.\nStrong duality: the primal optimum concentrates on the partition\nwhere the cost-to-coefficient ratio is minimised, which (for\nPlancherel cost) is the largest |y×χ| partition.\n\nThis is a THEOREM about LP structure, not about physics. It holds\nfor ANY LP with one equality constraint and non-negative costs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.dominantPartition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.dominantPartition","kind":"def","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/BindingConcentration.lean#L41-L52","doc":"The dominant binding partition for an atom with A nucleons on 3A strands.\nShape: (3A − k, k) where k = interfaceCount A = A−1.\n= (3A − (A−1), A−1) = (2A+1, A−1).\n\nD (A=2): (5,1), T (A=3): (7,2)... wait, empirically it's (6,3).\nThe formula needs correction — the interface count for A≥3 involves\nAVERAGED crossings which contribute differently. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.interfaceCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.BindingConcentration.interfaceCount","kind":"def","line":35,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/BindingConcentration.lean#L35-L39","doc":"The number of interface generators for an atom with A nucleons.\nEach nucleon occupies 3 strands; interfaces connect adjacent\nnucleon blocks at strand positions 3, 6, ..., 3(A-1).\nFor A nucleons: k = A−1 interface generators. ","signatureChunk":0}]}